## Why This Is Asked
Tests a candidate's ability to implement a straightforward streaming ingestion with validation, error handling, and rollback using familiar GCP primitives.

## Key Concepts
- Pub/Sub ingestion and Dataflow (Beam)
- JSON schema validation and field filtering
- BigQueryIO writes with insertId for idempotency and daily partitioning
- Dead-letter handling and Cloud Storage archival
- Lightweight testing and rollback planning

## Code Example
```javascript
function validate(record){
  if (typeof record.user_id !== 'string') return false;
  if (typeof record.event_time !== 'string') return false;
  if (typeof record.event_type !== 'string') return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle late-arriving data and schema evolution?
- What monitoring and alerting would you add for bad records and rollback validation?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical knowledge of end-to-end streaming ingestion on GCP with privacy and governance considerations. It evaluates the candidate's ability to apply core GCP primitives—including Pub/Sub, Dataflow, BigQuery, and Data Catalog—while addressing real-world concerns like multi-tenancy, data quality, and operational resilience.

## Key Concepts
- **Streaming Ingestion**: Pub/Sub for real-time event capture, Dataflow for scalable processing
- **Per-Tenant Isolation**: Shared raw table architecture with Authorized Views for secure multi-tenancy
- **Data Governance**: Data Catalog for schema registry and lineage tracking
- **Data Quality**: Schema enforcement, null validation, and statistical anomaly detection
- **Operational Concerns**: Late-arrival handling, rollback procedures, and schema evolution

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton DoFn to compute 3-sigma anomalies
class AnomalyDetectionDoFn extends DoFn {
  processElement(element) {
    const mean = this.meanField; // Pre-computed per tenant
    const stdDev = this.stdDevField;
    const threshold = 3 * stdDev;
    const value = element.numericField;
    const isAnomaly = Math.abs(value - mean) > threshold;
    // Output with anomaly flag
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would the design scale to hundreds of tenants while preserving isolation?
- What are the cost implications of the dead-letter queue approach for schema drift?
- How would you monitor data quality metrics in production?

## Why This Is Asked
This angle explores multi-tenant isolation, masking, and rollback in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Dataflow (Beam) streaming pipelines
- Pub/Sub for ingestion
- Per-tenant BigQuery datasets
- Data Loss Prevention masking in ingest
- Idempotent writes with MERGE
- Dead-letter queues and raw data archiving
- 24h rollback via replay from Cloud Storage
- Observability through Dataflow metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Dataflow masking sketch (pseudo)
class MaskPII {
  process(element) {
    if (element.email) element.email = '*****@*****.com';
    if (element.phone) element.phone = '**********';
    return element;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data quality across tenants? 
- How would you enforce per-tenant quotas in BigQuery and Dataflow?
